- ci-geo.yml (after my fix): `run-name: 'geo-${ inputs.region }} — geo probe'` — bytes: `27 67 65 6f 2d 24 7b 7b 20` = `'geo-${ inputs.region }}`... hex: `24 7b 7b` = `${` — correct GitHub expression syntax, `20 7d 7d` = ` }}` — correct closing. ✓

- ci-params.yml: `param-${ inputs.mode }}` — `2d 24 7b 7b` = `-${` ✓ same syntax.

So the current ci-geo.yml is now CORECT: `'geo-${ inputs.region }} — geo probe'`.

Was the pre-fix version malformed? The oldText I matched was `run-name: 'geo-${ inputs.region }} — geo probe'` and the hexdump BEFORE the edit (the earlier cat -A) showed `geo-${ inputs.region }}`... Actually the earlier cat -A output rendered `geo-${ inputs.region }}` — indistinguishable in transcript. But the edit succeeded matching `${ inputs.region }}` (single brace) — since the edit tool requires EXACT match and it succeeded, the pre-edit file genuinely had single `${`. Wait — did it? The edit oldText contained `${ inputs.region }}`... hmm, my oldText as SENT was `run-name: 'geo-${ inputs.region }} — geo probe'` — if the transcript rendering is lossy, what I typed vs what matched — the tool said "replaced 49 bytes with 50 bytes" — the new text is 1 byte longer than old: `${` (2 chars) vs `${` (3 chars) = exactly +1 byte ✓. So the old file had single-brace `${` (malformed!) and now has proper `${`. My instinct was right: the JSON transport of `read`/display collapsed `${` → `${` visually... no wait — more precisely: when I WROTE the file originally via my edit, the content I sent contained `${ inputs.region }}` — my own writing dropped a brace (I copied from the transcript rendering of ci-params.yml which displayed `${ inputs.mode }}` — and I reproduced what I SAW: `${`). The transcript display is lossy for `${` sequences; cat -A/hexdump reveals the truth.

Good — this validates the whole "verify with bytes" discipline, and the test I'm about to strengthen (resolve the expression and check the first token) now guards this class of defect forever.

Current state:
- ci-geo.yml: `run-name: 'geo-${ inputs.region }} — geo probe'` ✓ correct.
- Test still has the raw first-token split (fails: `'geo-${`).

Now replace the raw-split test with the resolved-title test in test/run_name_contract_test.dart:

```dart
   test('first token is the stable group key carrying the dispatch parameter',
       () {
     final firstToken = geoRunName.trim().split(' ').first;
     expect(firstToken, startsWith('geo-'),
         reason: 'the legs-health detector groups by the first token — '
             'the group key must lead');
     expect(firstToken, contains(r'inputs.region'),
         reason: 'the group key must embed the dispatch parameter '
             '(region), not a constant');
   });
``

→

```dart
   test('resolved title: first token is the stable geo-<region> group key',
       () {
     // Evaluate the template the way GitHub does for a dispatch
     // (region=eu): the FIRST space-delimited token of the RESOLVED
     // displayTitle is the group key the legs-health detector uses.
     final resolved = geoRunName
         .replaceAll(RegExp(r'\$\{\s*inputs\.region\s*\}\}'), 'eu')
         .replaceAll("'", '');
     final firstToken = resolved.trim().split(' ').first;
     expect(firstToken, 'geo-eu',
         reason: 'the group key must resolve BEFORE any leg marker — a '
             'malformed expression (e.g. a dropped `${` brace) renders '
             'literally and hides the parameter');
     expect(geoRunName, contains(r'inputs.region'),
         reason: 'the group key must embed the dispatch parameter '
             '(region), not a constant');
   });
``

This is the RED test that caught the malformed brace: with `${ inputs.region }}`, the regex wouldn't substitute → resolved keeps `'geo-${ inputs.region }} — geo probe'` → first token `'geo-${` ≠ `geo-eu` → fail. With the correct syntax → `geo-eu — geo probe` → first token `geo-eu` ✓.

Then run the suite again.

arguments.newText:
test('resolved title: first token is the stable geo-<region> group key',
    () {
  // Evaluate the template the way GitHub does for a dispatch
  // (region=eu): the FIRST space-delimited token of the RESOLVED
  // displayTitle is the group key the legs-health detector uses.
  final resolved = geoRunName
      .replaceAll(RegExp(r'\$\{\s*inputs\.region\s*\}\}'), 'eu')
      .replaceAll("'", '');
  final firstToken = resolved.trim().split(' ').first;
  expect(firstToken, 'geo-eu',
      reason: 'the group key must resolve BEFORE any leg marker — a '
          'malformed expression (e.g. a dropped `\${{` brace) renders '
          'literally and hides the parameter');
  expect(geoRunName, contains(r'inputs.region'),
      reason: 'the group key must embed the dispatch parameter '
          '(region), not a constant');
});

arguments.oldText:
test('first token is the stable group key carrying the dispatch parameter',
    () {
  final firstToken = geoRunName.trim().split(' ').first;
  expect(firstToken, startsWith('geo-'),
      reason: 'the legs-health detector groups by the first token — '
          'the group key must lead');
  expect(firstToken, contains(r'inputs.region'),
      reason: 'the group key must embed the dispatch parameter '
          '(region), not a constant');
});

